This example demonstrates a Path Traversal (or Directory Traversal) vulnerability. This occurs when an application uses unsanitized user input to construct a file path, allowing an attacker to access files and directories stored outside the intended base directory.

```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;

/**
 * A class demonstrating improper API use for file access.
 */
public class UnsafeFileAccessor {

    // The intended directory where user files are stored.
    private static final String BASE_DIRECTORY = "/var/www/uploads/";

    /**
     * Reads a file based on a user-provided filename.
     * @param filename The name of the file to read, provided by the user.
     * @return The byte content of the file.
     * @throws IOException If the file cannot be read.
     */
    public byte[] getFileContent(String filename) throws IOException {
        // This concatenation is vulnerable to Path Traversal.
        // An attacker could provide a filename like "../../../etc/passwd" to read
        // sensitive system files outside the BASE_DIRECTORY.
        File requestedFile = new File(BASE_DIRECTORY + filename);

        // The application reads and returns whatever file the path points to.
        return Files.readAllBytes(requestedFile.toPath());
    }
}
